Nick Doty said:

@Martin, I do mean timing, and maybe I shouldn't lump that in as "traffic analysis", but I admit that I often do

Ack.  Timing issues are a concern and I won't pretend there is a perfect answer, but I think that we have some pretty strong defenses.

Daniel Gillmor said:

@Martin, agreed that this is a "harm reduction" approach, but what keeps sites from demanding this and then still doing the other kinds of abuse

What we are hoping to do is to clamp down more aggressively on sites that engage in "anti-abuse" actions that we see as harmful to privacy: fingerprinting, identification, etc...

Nick Doty said:

@Usama, oh, I'm sorry, I wasn't trying to comment on your question in particular, just that we were going through a lot of protocol questions in the chat

Sorry for my misunderstanding, too.

Nick Doty said:

@mirja, I think the endorsement concept can be fully separated from the credential/credit system. and maybe that's good for simplifying? but I do think there are plausible reasons why the system will work better (for privacy and openness) if both are available

Yes focusing on the endorsement first seems already have a use.

Watson Ladd said:

@Steve: future behavior is harder to vouch for than linking to a scarce resource. I think anchors can manage the second but not the first

yes

Benjamin VanderSloot said:

@Eric: probabalistic anchor holdouts I think is the strongest mitigation I see there, however it is not clear if it would be sufficient. The anchor sets would be the key piece to know if it would work

Which was why I think it's so important that there be some concrete plan for there to be non-aligned anchors
